          431.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "8e9ecccfa33c7a0d0bca2d7a8a2164cbab7fd91bf7473259c314183b6bb2c86e"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "With such an experimental design, an approximate $t$ test can be constructed for the null hypothesis of no inbreeding depression. Here we take the null model to be one of purely additive gene action. Consider the situation in which $n$ progeny are assayed from each of $L$ independent families, both in the control and in the inbred population. Under the null hypothesis of no inbreeding depression, the difference between the observed mean phenotypes of noninbred and inbred offspring ($\\Delta\\bar{z} = \\bar{z}_{O} - \\bar{z}_{I}$) has expectation zero, and the observed difference must be evaluated against its sampling variance, the sum of the variances of $\\bar{z}_{O}$ and $\\bar{z}_{I}$. Several factors contribute to this variance, as can be seen by referring to the definition of the sample mean",
      "source_page": 276,
      "printed_page": 256,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter10:p010:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        128.0,
        429.0,
        1089.0,
        753.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        276
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
