      "content_sha256": "2aba989422e33a0fbfa8eba090f72ef0c564c446a65209a46d141b931dc9491c"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "We start by considering the control. First, the variance in the control mean caused by environmental effects specific to individuals is $ \\sigma_{e}^{2}/(Ln) $ because all such effects are distributed independently among the Ln individuals. Second, the variance caused by maternal (or general environmental) effects is $ \\sigma_{E}^{2}/L $; it is only divided by L because L mothers contribute to the control mean. Third, because the segregational variance within families in the random-mating base population is $ \\sigma^{2}(a_{ij}) = \\sigma_{A}^{2}/2 $, and the effects of such residual variation are random with respect to individuals, the contribution of the within-family genetic variance to the variance of the mean is $ \\sigma_{A}^{2}/(2Ln) $. (To ease the passage through this difficult area, we give this and a few other results without proof.) Finally, the among-family variance, $ \\sigma^{2}(A_{i}) $, is also $ \\sigma_{A}^{2}/2 $, and it contributes $ \\sigma_{A}^{2}/(2L) $ to the sampling variance of the control mean. Summing up terms, the variance of the control mean phenotype is",
      "source_page": 276,
      "printed_page": 256,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter10:p010:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
